WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980
Good morning Hank it's Tuesday.

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.340
Thanks for your excellent video about  Hillary Clinton and Bernie Sanders' tax plans

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.780
Today I'm gonna discuss the tax plans put forth by Donald Trump and Ted Cruz.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.680
I would talk about John Kasich's tax plan,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.600
but he hasn't put out a detailed one yet, so I can't.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.900
Ok but first, a brief overview of the federal budget for which these taxes pay.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.860
About a third of the US's 3.8 trillion dollar federal budget goes to Social Security and unemployment,

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.640
mostly to Social security, which provides income for elderly and disabled people.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.340
27% goes to healthcare spending.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.320
This is mostly medicare, our publicly-funded health insurance program for the elderly.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.940
16% goes to the military, 6% to service the national debt, 4% to veteran's benefits,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.900
and after that, the pieces of the pie start to get pretty small.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.460
So Hank, as you pointed out last week,

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.500
[Hank talking] Taxes aren't any fun.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.460
They are also very, very complicated,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.980
and I want to emphasize that I am neither a tax policy expert nor an economist.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:52.460
Although in 1994, I did win the bronze medal in economics at the Alabama State Academic Decathlon.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:58.000
So for one brief shining moment, I was the third-best economist of all seventeen-year-old C students in the state of Alabama.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.800
but I recognize that's not expertise.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.560
I have, however, read a lot of experts.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.400
Ok, so both Donald Trump and Ted Cruz have proposed tax refoms that will dramatically simplify the American tax code.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.780
in fact, Cruz has promised to make taxation so straightforward that we won't even need an Internal Revenue Service anymore,

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.740
so let's start there with his plan.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.900
So right now in the US we have seven tax brackets,

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.740
so like if you're married and filing jointly, and you made a million dollars last year,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.300
first off, congratulations.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.280
but as for your taxes, the first 18,450 of your million dollars are taxed at 10%.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.560
The next 56,449 dollars are taxed at 15%,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:39.340
et cetera, with everything over 465-ish thousand dollars taxed at the top US marginal tax rate of 39.6%.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.000
Except not, actually, because of deductions.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.240
Like if you have a mortgage, you can deduct the interest you pay, money you give to charity can be deducted as well,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.460
your student loan interest might be deductible, some retirement savings, some childcare costs, lots of other stuff.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:55.200
But let's imagine an extremely unlikely scenario where you're making a million dollars a year,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.580
but don't claim any of these deductions. You can still claim the so-called 'standard deduction'.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:07.080
This is a tax deduction available to everyone. If you're married and filing jointly, it's currently $12,600 per year.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:12.220
So your first $12,600 of income wouldn't be taxed regardless. In the end, with a million dollars of income,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:20.080
taking only the standard deduction, you'd pay about $343,427 in tax, an affective tax rate of like, 34.3%.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:27.420
The same family earning $250,000 a year would pay $53,755, an effective tax rate of 21.5%.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.020
And if your family had the median household income of about $52,000, you'd pay just under

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.840
$5,000 in federal income taxes, an effective tax rate of about 9.6%.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.640
Ted Cruz's plan is to collapse these seven tax brackets into a single 10% tax rate for all income.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.920
This is sometimes called a flat tax. Crucially, Cruz would also increase the standard deduction

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.680
to $20,000 a year for married couples filing jointly. So the median US household income would

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.200
end up paying $3,200 a year in federal taxes instead of 5,000, a reduction of $1,800, or 34%.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:06.120
The family making a million dollars a year would pay $98,000 a year in taxes, a reduction of $245,000 a year, or 72%.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.900
So everyone would pay less tax, but the wealthiest Americans would see, by far, the biggest cuts,

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.040
both in percentage and absolute dollars.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.040
Cruz would also eliminate most of the aforementioned tax deductions, except for mortgage interest and charitable giving.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:23.820
He would also eliminate all federal gift and estate taxes, and repeal all corporate and payroll taxes,

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.040
in favour of a 16% kind-of VAT, which is where it gets really interesting.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.440
Insofar that this stuff can be interesting. By the way, Europeans were like, 'oh my god, he finally said a word I recognize, VAT!'

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.480
Kinda. In most European countries, there's a broad tax on consumption called the VAT or value-added tax

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.580
Cruz's VAT is a little different. Basically, it taxes businesses revenues minus their capital investments

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.720
and the money they pay to other companies. I'm gonna oversimplify here a little bit, but imagine I wanted to

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.960
start a company selling T-shirts that celebrated my love of pizza, that were available for $20 right now at dftba.com.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.500
Now imagine that I had to pay a company $10 to manufacture each T-shirt.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:03.660
I would only pay VAT on the $10 difference. The other company would pay VAT on the $10 that I payed them.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.000
And then they would get to subtract any non-wage costs they incurred making the shirt, et cetera.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.120
Also, if I wanted to buy my own machine to make T-shirts, I could subtract the cost of that machine

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.280
from my revenue before paying the VAT. The money left over is either profit that goes to owners,

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.560
or wages that go to employees. So basically, it's a tax on both wages and corporate profits, and it would

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:26.860
aim to do a better job than our current system does of actually, like, taxing economic activity.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:31.000
Which would be good, because, as you might've heard, with the current system, there's a fair bit of tax avoidance going on.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.940
Lots of economists, although certainly not all of them, like the idea of this kind of VAT.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.440
But Cruz's specific plan would dramatically reduce the amount of federal revenue.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.840
Like, the nonpartisan Tax Policy Center calculates that Cruz's plan would reduce federal revenue by 8.6 trillion

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:49.820
dollars over the next ten years. Now, Cruz says that by eliminating the IRS and a few other federal agencies,

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:54.840
the government can save 500 billion dollars over the next decade, but that's less than 10% the shortfall,.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:00.180
Like, if you ceased all military spending in the United States, you still be 2 trillion dollars short.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.280
But Cruz doesn't want to eliminate military spending, instead he wants to increase it dramatically.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.420
Now, some economists argue the shortfall will partly be made up by increased economic growth,

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:13.280
but even the most optimistic independent modelling I could find still showed a dramatic increase in the

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:20.080
budget deficit under a Cruz tax plan, barring huge cuts to either Medicare, or Social Security, or some combination of the two.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.680
Okay, so then there's Donald Trump's plan. Trump would also increase the standard deduction,

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:29.280
but by more, so that any couple making $50,000 a year or less would pay no federal income tax.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:36.280
And he would collapse the 7 tax brackets into 4, with the top rate being 25% on income over $432,000 per year.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:41.060
Trump's plan is similar to Cruz's in that he would also eliminate gift and estate taxes, and most deductions.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:44.680
But he would cut corporate tax rates rather than replace them with a VAT,  which would probably lead to a lot

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:49.880
of people identifying as corporations instead of human beings for complicated reasons explained here if you wanna pause the video.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:53.500
The other facet of Trump's tax plan that he talks a lot about on the campaign trailers that currently

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:55.500
hedgefund managers and some of the other people who currently work in finance

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.340
can be paid in what's called, carried interest,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.560
which allows them to pay a lower tax rate. And Trump's plan would end this.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.260
But, because income tax rates would be so low under the Trump plan

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.040
almost all hedgefund managers will pay less tax than they do now

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.040
While campaigning, Trump has also repeatedly said that

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:12.760
his tax tax plan will quote, "cost me a fortune"

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.820
which is just not true.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.440
Like under the Trump plan, if you make a million dollars a year

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.980
you'll pay about $200,000 a year in taxes, which is much higher than under the Cruz plan

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.180
but much, much lower than the current rates.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.600
But altogether, Trump's proposed tax plan would actually cost the federal government

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:31.020
more revenue than Cruz's plan, somewhere between 10 and 12 trillion dollars

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:32.120
over the next decade.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:35.240
That's about like, uh, 25% of the total federal budget.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.640
He has said that his tax policy will be revenue-neutral, but in response to that

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.660
the right-leaning center for federal tax policy wrote an op-ed entitled,

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.780
"Donald Trump's Tax Plan Will Not Be Revenue-Neutral Under Any Circumstances"

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.680
The tax policy center, meanwhile, estimates that eliminating the United States Military all together

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:55.120
would cover just about half of the revenue gap created by Trump's tax plan.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.780
So in summary, Ted Cruz's tax plan involves a radical simplification of the tax code.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:05.080
A flat 10% income tax would lead to lower taxes for all Americans, but the wealthy would benefit the most

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.600
And Cruz's strategy for revamping the corporate tax code has support among a lot of economists,

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.800
but the low rates would lead to dramatically higher budget-deficits.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.060
Trump's tax plan would probably lead to even bigger deficits.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:20.280
As with Cruz's tax plan, everyone would pay lower taxes, but the rich get the majority of the benefit.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.020
Although, Trump's tax plan does not benefit the rich as much as Cruz's does.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:25.140
But more importantly,

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.760
neither of these plans is at all feasible.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.820
Unless, we completely reimagine the role that government plays in American lives.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:35.380
Especially the lives of the elderly, the disabled, and military veterans.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:39.960
Budget cuts to services benefitting those people are extremely politically unpopular,

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:44.040
so much so, that both candidates have promised to find away to preserve those programs.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:49.520
But to speak frankly, neither of these candidates has offered a serious explanation for how they would do that.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.920
You simply cannot cut government receipts by 20%

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:56.760
while preserving the programs that together make up more than 80% of the budget.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:58.700
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

